{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791557928,"nonce":"NMTUZ+g9MK0fezPUB+opQvNJLtg=","title":"Memory: what Ash did to cut idle and steady-state footprint, and where WrenLift stands","message":"Ash (rayzor-blade/ash, 2026-09-19 .. 10-09) cut memory in three places. Below, each item says what Ash did, then where WrenLift stands, with the files involved.\n\nBaseline, this Mac, wlift built with llvm,aot (89 MB binary), measured by ps RSS and footprint's phys_footprint:\n- A program that prints a line and sleeps: RSS 12 MB, phys_footprint 4.5 MB, in both interpreter and tiered mode.\n- 2M short-lived strings (20 kept), then a sleep: interpreter RSS 38 MB with phys_footprint 31 MB; tiered RSS 56 MB with phys_footprint 27 MB, held for the whole sleep.\n\n\nAsh:\n- b57fae8: a background thread runs a major collection once every mutator has been in a declared blocking wait for 300 ms, with \u003e= 8 MB allocated since the last collection. Idleness is reckoned across waits, so repeated short waits count, with run time \u003c= 5% of the span. An idle collection counts as quiet, so it returns pages. ASH_GC_IDLE=0 turns it off.\n- 781ebbb: freed GC pages that a busy collection kept are returned once the mutators go idle.\n- 3cd9351: when idle, after malloc's use has fallen 4 MB from its peak, Ash calls malloc_trim on Linux and malloc_zone_pressure_relief on macOS. Compile threads and native code free their scratch after the last collection, which an idle program never follows with another.\n- 7b0f406: native memory owned by finalizable objects counts toward the trigger.\n\nWrenLift: the collector is Ash's earlier design (src/runtime/gc_immix_heap.rs).\n- HEARTBEAT is 30 s. A collection is quiet if it comes HEARTBEAT after the last one, and only a quiet collection runs hand_back_free_blocks.\n- RESIDENT_FLOAT is 16 blocks.\n- The trigger is allocation-only: threshold = live x WLIFT_GC_GROWTH (default 4), clamped between a floor and a ceiling.\n- So a burst below the threshold followed by idle keeps its garbage until something allocates; that is the 27-31 MB in the baseline.\n\nTo do:\n- Idle collection driven by WrenLift's declared waits: sched::idle (src/runtime/sched.rs, the Condvar wait), TimeCore.sleep, channel receive and isolate join, and a host's event-loop wait through a C API call such as wlift_blocking(bool).\n- An idle collection is quiet: it returns pages, and then trims malloc. The release binary uses the system allocator (llvm,aot features), so malloc_trim and malloc_zone_pressure_relief apply as they do in Ash.\n- Native memory: only krio stacks call track_external_alloc (fiber.rs). A foreign object's native buffers (image, sqlite, gpu, zip plugins) add nothing to the trigger, so a loop making native handles need never collect. Add a C API for a foreign class to charge its bytes, released by the finalizer.\n\n\nAsh:\n- 2d490e0 frees an OSR entry's and own-module promotion's IR after codegen.\n- 6fde0a9 (open) found about 34 MB of LLVM IR operands still live 50 s into a run, because MCJIT keeps each module's IR beside its code.\n- f60b855 (open) found the optimised AIR cached per function for the whole run, 50-74 MB in app workloads.\n- 69f3fa3 keeps application workloads in Cranelift until they are hot for longer, so fewer LLVM compiles happen.\n\nWrenLift:\n- src/codegen/llvm_backend.rs LlvmCompiledCode owns its ExecutionEngine and Context (_engine, _context) for as long as the code lives, so every LLVM-tier function keeps its whole module's IR. Dropping the IR while keeping the code is the change: delete function bodies after the code is finalised, or load objects through a memory manager without keeping the module.\n- src/runtime/engine.rs FuncBody keeps mir: Arc\u003cMirFunction\u003e and the bytecode for every function, compiled or not, for the whole run. A MIR instruction is 64 bytes, (ValueId, Instruction) is 72 with alignment, a BasicBlock 144 and a MirFunction 376.\n  - Ash's next target is shrinking its 40-byte opcode or decoding lazily.\n  - Options for WrenLift: drop a natively compiled function's MIR when nothing can deopt into it, or keep it compact.\n- Measure first: a heap profile (MallocStackLogging=lite + malloc_history on macOS, heaptrack on the NUC) of a hatch app and the site benches in tiered mode, totalled by owner.\n\n\nAsh, issue d0ca1c3: idle RSS on Linux was 44-58 MB against stock HashLink's 4 MB, nearly all of it file-backed pages of the 142 MB executable. The statically linked LLVM runs about 650 static constructors at start-up, and 64 KB fault-around pulls in neighbouring pages.\n- 5aa28fd: lld --symbol-ordering-file, generated from Intel PT traces of start-up, lays out start-up code together. interp 44.7 -\u003e 18.3 MB.\n- 196ee65: -z pack-relative-relocs where the build host's glibc is \u003e= 2.36. .rela.dyn 3.0 MB -\u003e 128 KB; interp 18.3 -\u003e 15.6.\n- 2442d6d: LLVM's static constructors move out of .init_array (a linker script) and run on first use. A run that promotes nothing never touches LLVM; interp -\u003e 12.9, hybrid 19.1 -\u003e 13.7.\n\nWrenLift: the release wlift links LLVM statically and now also wasmtime 43 (the aot feature, for wlift program.wasm), 89 MB on macOS. The Mac idle RSS is low (12 MB). Linux has not been measured.\n- Measure on the NUC: /proc/\u003cpid\u003e/smaps_rollup for an idle wlift --mode interpreter and tiered, anonymous against file-backed.\n- If it shows Ash's shape, the same three changes apply in build.rs (which already passes linker arguments for the export lists). LLVM is used only by the top tier and AOT, so lazy constructors fit WrenLift's ladder too.\n- Also weigh moving the wasm host (wasmtime) out of the default wlift, or measure what it adds.\n\n\n- 82cfdcf: runtime helpers allocate from the thread's bump region instead of under the global GC lock.\n- add01fe: address-keyed type tables are hashed by multiply-fold, not SipHash.\n- 3b72a18: an interpreted method call allocates nothing per call.\n- WrenLift should check its own interpreter call path for per-call Vec allocation in the same heap profile.","files":null}]}